     1	# Context Efficiency Comparison: MCP vs Mini-Query DSL vs Board Grep
     2	
     3	**Date:** 2026-02-11
     4	**Board:** `/Users/aagrigore1/src/skill-project-management/.task-board/`
     5	**Board size:** 18 epics, 69 stories, 244 tasks, 15 bugs (346 elements total)
     6	
     7	---
     8	
     9	## 1. Methodology
    10	
    11	### Measurement approach
    12	
    13	1. **Live execution** against the real `.task-board/` directory (346 elements, mostly in `done` status).
    14	2. **Output bytes** measured by piping command output through `wc -c`.
    15	3. **ANSI stripping** applied to CLI text output (`perl -pe 's/\e\[\d+m//g'`) since LLMs process the raw byte stream including escape codes, but they carry zero semantic information.
    16	4. **Token estimation** using standard ratios:
    17	   - English text: **1 token per 4 bytes**
    18	   - JSON output: **1 token per 3 bytes** (more punctuation, shorter words)
    19	   - JSON schemas/tool definitions: **1 token per 3 bytes**
    20	5. **MCP and DSL produce identical JSON** for the same field selections -- both use the shared `internal/fields` package. MCP output measurements are therefore equal to DSL output measurements.
    21	6. **Input overhead** measured as byte length of the command string (Bash) or JSON input body (MCP).
    22	7. **Per-call framing** estimated at ~80 tokens per tool round-trip (50 tokens request frame + 30 tokens result frame). This is identical for Bash and MCP tool calls.
    23	
    24	### What is NOT identical: session overhead
    25	
    26	- **MCP**: 12 tool definitions loaded into the agent's context at session start. This is a one-time cost (~2,200 tokens) that persists for the entire session.
    27	- **DSL/Grep via Bash**: Zero additional tool definitions. The Bash tool is already part of the agent's base system prompt.
    28	- **CLI via Bash**: Same as DSL/Grep -- zero additional definitions.
    29	
    30	---
    31	
    32	## 2. Raw Measurements
    33	
    34	### Output sizes (response tokens consumed by the agent reading the result)
    35	
    36	| Workflow | CLI (text) | DSL (JSON) | MCP (JSON) | Grep (text) |
    37	|----------|-----------|-----------|-----------|------------|
    38	| **W1: Orientation** (`summary`) | 714 B / ~179 tok | 1,099 B / ~366 tok | 1,099 B / ~366 tok | 1,818 B / ~455 tok |
    39	| **W2: Task lookup** (default) | 657 B / ~164 tok | 114 B / ~38 tok | 114 B / ~38 tok | 0 B (task not in file content) |
    40	| **W2: Task lookup** (full) | 657 B / ~164 tok | 907 B / ~302 tok | 907 B / ~302 tok | N/A |
    41	| **W2: Task lookup** (minimal) | 657 B / ~164 tok | 60 B / ~20 tok | 60 B / ~20 tok | N/A |
    42	| **W3: Filtered list** (minimal) | 285 B / ~71 tok | 114 B / ~38 tok | 114 B / ~38 tok | 1,346 B / ~337 tok |
    43	| **W3: Filtered list** (overview) | 285 B / ~71 tok | 246 B / ~82 tok | 246 B / ~82 tok | 1,346 B / ~337 tok |
    44	| **W4: Agent monitoring** | 1,552 B / ~388 tok | 1,795 B / ~598 tok | 1,795 B / ~598 tok | 44,084 B / ~11,021 tok |
    45	| **W5: Batch (3 tasks, status only)** | 2,173 B / ~543 tok | 103 B / ~34 tok | 3x ~60 B = ~180 B / ~60 tok | 1,045 B / ~261 tok |
    46	
    47	### Input sizes (tokens the agent spends to formulate the request)
    48	
    49	| Workflow | CLI | DSL | MCP | Grep |
    50	|----------|-----|-----|-----|------|
    51	| **W1** | 18 B / ~5 tok | 24 B / ~6 tok | 20 B / ~5 tok | 66 B / ~17 tok |
    52	| **W2** (default) | 34 B / ~9 tok | 50 B / ~13 tok | 28 B / ~7 tok | 41 B / ~10 tok |
    53	| **W3** (minimal) | 42 B / ~11 tok | 62 B / ~16 tok | 64 B / ~16 tok | 48 B / ~12 tok |
    54	| **W4** | 17 B / ~4 tok | 31 B / ~8 tok | 20 B / ~5 tok | 48 B / ~12 tok |
    55	| **W5** (3 tasks) | 110 B / ~28 tok | 121 B / ~30 tok | 3x 50 B = 150 B / ~38 tok | 116 B / ~29 tok |
    56	
    57	### Total tokens per workflow (input + output + per-call framing at 80 tok/call)
    58	
    59	| Workflow | CLI | DSL | MCP | Grep |
    60	|----------|-----|-----|-----|------|
    61	| **W1: Orientation** | 264 | 452 | 451 | 552 |
    62	| **W2: Task lookup** (default) | 253 | 131 | 125 | N/A (fails) |
    63	| **W2: Task lookup** (minimal) | 253 | 113 | 107 | N/A |
    64	| **W3: Filtered list** (minimal) | 162 | 134 | 134 | 429 |
    65	| **W4: Agent monitoring** | 472 | 686 | 683 | 11,113 |
    66	| **W5: Batch** (status) | 811 (3 calls x 270) | 144 | 338 (3 calls x 113) | 610 (3 calls x 203) |
    67	
    68	**Notes:**
    69	- CLI counts include 3 separate Bash calls for W5 (3x framing overhead).
    70	- MCP counts include 3 separate MCP calls for W5 (3x framing overhead).
    71	- DSL handles W5 in a single Bash call (1x framing overhead).
    72	- Grep fails entirely for W2 (task ID is in directory name, not file content).
    73	- Grep's W4 result (44 KB) is catastrophically large -- it matches every `progress.md` header.
    74	
    75	---
    76	
    77	## 3. Session Overhead
    78	
    79	### MCP: one-time tool definition cost
    80	
    81	| Component | Bytes | Tokens |
    82	|-----------|-------|--------|
    83	| 12 tool definitions (names, descriptions, JSON schemas) | ~7,600 B | ~2,200 tok |
    84	| **Read-only tools** (5 tools: get_element, list, summary, plan, agents) | ~2,448 B | ~815 tok |
    85	| **Write tools** (7 tools: update_status, create, assign, unassign, progress, link, search) | ~3,327 B | ~1,109 tok |
    86	| **Total loaded per session** | ~7,621 B | ~2,200 tok |
    87	
    88	### DSL: zero additional overhead
    89	
    90	The DSL adds no tool definitions. The agent already has the Bash tool. It just needs to know the query syntax, which can be provided in the system prompt or discovered with `task-board q --help`.
    91	
    92	If we include DSL syntax documentation in the system prompt (the `--help` text is ~500 bytes), that's ~125 tokens -- about 18x smaller than MCP tool definitions.
    93	
    94	### Grep: zero additional overhead
    95	
    96	Same as DSL. No tool definitions. Usage is discoverable via `task-board grep --help` (~200 bytes / ~50 tokens).
    97	
    98	### CLI: zero additional overhead
    99	
   100	Same. No tool definitions.
   101	
   102	---
   103	
   104	## 4. Break-Even Analysis
   105	
   106	The question: **At how many queries does MCP's session overhead (2,200 tokens) pay for itself vs DSL?**
   107	
   108	### Per-query savings: MCP vs DSL
   109	
   110	MCP and DSL produce identical JSON output. The only per-query differences are:
   111	
   112	| Factor | MCP | DSL | Delta |
   113	|--------|-----|-----|-------|
   114	| Input size | Slightly smaller (JSON vs command string) | Slightly larger | ~5-10 tokens MCP advantage |
   115	| Output size | Identical | Identical | 0 |
   116	| Framing | Same (~80 tok) | Same (~80 tok) | 0 |
   117	| Batch capability | No (one call per operation) | Yes (semicolons in single call) | DSL advantage for batches |
   118	
   119	**Single-query delta:** MCP saves ~5-10 tokens per query on input.
   120	
   121	**Break-even at:** 2,200 / 7.5 = **~293 queries per session**
   122	
   123	For batch queries (W5-style), the math reverses:
   124	- DSL batch of 3: 144 tokens (1 call)
   125	- MCP batch of 3: 338 tokens (3 calls)
   126	- **DSL saves ~194 tokens per 3-element batch**
   127	
   128	With batching factored in, MCP **never breaks even** against DSL for typical agent sessions.
   129	
   130	### MCP vs CLI
   131	
   132	CLI text output is sometimes smaller (W1: 179 vs 366 tokens) and sometimes larger (W5: 543 vs 34 tokens). The key advantage of structured approaches (MCP/DSL) is **field selection** -- the agent can request only what it needs.
   133	
   134	For field-selected queries:
   135	- `get(X) { minimal }` = 20 tokens vs `show X` = 164 tokens
   136	- Savings: **144 tokens per lookup**
   137	- Break-even for MCP vs CLI: 2,200 / 144 = **~15 queries per session**
   138	
   139	### MCP vs Grep
   140	
   141	Grep is so unreliable (fails for W2, 11K tokens for W4) that break-even analysis is meaningless. Grep loses on every workflow where the board has more than a few elements.
   142	
   143	---
   144	
   145	## 5. Recommendation
   146	
   147	### Primary recommendation: **Mini-Query DSL**
   148	
   149	The DSL is the optimal approach for all measured workflows:
   150	
   151	| Criterion | DSL | MCP | CLI | Grep |
   152	|-----------|-----|-----|-----|------|
   153	| Session overhead | None | ~2,200 tok | None | None |
   154	| Field selection | Yes | Yes | No | No |
   155	| Batch support | Yes (single call) | No (N calls) | No (N calls) | No (N calls) |
   156	| Output format | Structured JSON | Structured JSON | Human text + ANSI | Raw file:line:content |
   157	| Reliability | High | High | High | Low (content-only search) |
   158	| Discovery | `--help` in prompt | Tool definitions auto-loaded | `--help` in prompt | `--help` in prompt |
   159	
   160	### When to use each approach
   161	
   162	**DSL (default for all agent workflows):**
   163	- Any read operation: orientation, lookup, filtering, monitoring, batch queries
   164	- Particularly dominant for batch operations (single call vs N calls)
   165	- Best for sessions with mixed read patterns
   166	
   167	**MCP (specific niche: long-lived sessions with external tool orchestration):**
   168	- When the agent platform natively supports MCP and tool switching overhead matters more than context size
   169	- When the session is so long that 2,200 tokens of overhead becomes negligible
   170	- When other MCP servers are already in use (marginal cost of adding one more is lower)
   171	
   172	**CLI (human-facing output or quick checks):**
   173	- When a human is reading the output directly
   174	- When ANSI formatting aids comprehension
   175	- Not recommended for agent-to-agent communication
   176	
   177	**Grep (last resort for unstructured exploration):**
   178	- Searching for text patterns that span multiple elements (e.g., "who mentioned X in their notes?")
   179	- Discovering elements by content rather than by ID or status
   180	- Never for structured queries that the DSL handles natively
   181	
   182	---
   183	
   184	## 6. Summary
   185	
   186	The Mini-Query DSL is the clear winner for agent workflows: it has zero session overhead (vs MCP's 2,200 tokens), supports batch queries in a single tool call (saving ~194 tokens per 3-element batch vs MCP's 3 separate calls), and produces the same structured JSON output as MCP. Board Grep is a niche tool for unstructured text search that fails catastrophically on structured queries (11K tokens for agent monitoring vs DSL's 686). MCP only becomes competitive in sessions exceeding ~300 queries, a threshold that typical agent tasks never reach.
   187	
   188	---
   189	
   190	## Appendix: Token Estimation Calibration
   191	
   192	Token estimates used in this document are approximations. Actual token counts vary by model tokenizer. The ratios used:
   193	
   194	| Content type | Bytes per token | Rationale |
   195	|-------------|-----------------|-----------|
   196	| English text (CLI output) | 4.0 | Standard English tokenization |
   197	| JSON (DSL/MCP output) | 3.0 | More punctuation characters, shorter key names |
   198	| JSON Schema (tool definitions) | 3.0 | Same as JSON |
   199	| Command strings | 4.0 | Mix of English words and identifiers |
   200	
   201	For production use, these should be validated against the actual tokenizer (cl100k_base for Claude models).

