articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:95:This is the fundamental structural property of schema-once formats: field names are declared once, then never repeated. Abbreviating a one-time declaration yields a one-time saving. As payload size grows, the marginal benefit of that saving approaches zero.
articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:235:**Batching comparison:** For the "check status of 5 tasks" workflow, batching 5 queries into a single call costs 165 tokens — 33% less than individual queries (246 tok) and 46% less than aliases (306 tok). Batching is the superior optimization with zero overhead.
articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:249:> **In any schema-once output format (CSV, TSV, TOON), field names appear exactly once. Abbreviating a one-time occurrence produces a one-time saving. As payload size grows, the marginal value of that saving approaches zero.**
articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:263:Optimizations 1-4 work from the first query with zero discovery overhead. Aliases are the only optimization requiring upfront investment (schema roundtrip), and the investment exceeds the return in most scenarios.
articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:267:A second-order insight: modern BPE tokenizers already perform the compression that aliases attempt. Common field names — "status", "name", "id", "type" — are single tokens in cl100k_base. Abbreviating `status` (1 token) to `s` (1 token) saves zero tokens. Only multi-token field names benefit: `blocked_by` (3 tokens) -> `bb` (1 token) saves 2 tokens. But these savings only manifest in JSON (where keys repeat per item), not in compact format (where the header appears once).
articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:296:The token optimization hierarchy for agent-facing output is clear: field projection and compact format deliver the largest gains (46%+) with zero overhead. Batching and presets provide additional savings. Aliases sit at the bottom — the only optimization where discovery cost exceeds the benefit in typical usage.
articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:310:2. **Batch query utilization** — agents underutilize multi-query syntax, leaving ~80 tokens per call on the table
SKILL.md:3:description: Design pattern for building agent-optimized CLI query layers. Two-layer approach — mini-query DSL for structured reads (field projection, batching) + scoped grep for full-text search. Minimizes token overhead, eliminates MCP session cost.
SKILL.md:24:- Need to minimize tokens per query without adding infrastructure (MCP servers, HTTP APIs)
SKILL.md:25:- Want batch queries in a single tool call
SKILL.md:43:### Why not MCP?
SKILL.md:45:MCP loads tool definitions into the agent's context at session start. For a typical tool with 10-15 operations, that's ~2,000-3,000 tokens of dead weight per session. The DSL approach costs zero — the agent calls Bash with a query string.
SKILL.md:47:Per-query, MCP and DSL produce identical output when backed by the same field selection engine. But DSL supports batching (multiple queries in one call), while MCP requires separate tool calls. MCP never breaks even for typical agent sessions.
SKILL.md:49:See [references/comparison-example.md](references/comparison-example.md) for a real-world measurement on a 346-element board.
SKILL.md:85:- [ ] Support batching via `;` separator
SKILL.md:407:| MCP server for simple read queries | ~2,200 tok session overhead, no batching | DSL subcommand |
SKILL.md:409:| No batching support | 3 lookups = 3 tool calls = 3x framing overhead | Semicolon-separated queries |
SKILL.md:445:Key: `agentquery.Schema[T]` is the single source of truth for fields, presets, operations, and mutations. If you add MCP later, it uses the same Schema — identical output guaranteed.
SKILL.md:462:batch     = query (";" query)*
SKILL.md:477:3. **Batch savings:** compare N separate calls vs 1 batched call (N-1 fewer framing overheads at ~80 tok each)
SKILL.md:478:4. **Session overhead:** should be zero (no tool definitions loaded)
SKILL.md:480:Target: **30-50% token reduction** on reads vs CLI text output, **50%+ reduction** on batch operations.
SKILL.md:501:- [Real-world comparison: MCP vs DSL vs Grep](references/comparison-example.md) — measured on a 346-element task board, covers 5 workflows with exact token counts
